# 185 · A run's work in git: a branch for its champions, one for each lane, and new campaigns from a champion's commit

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.1.1](../roadmap.md#v011)

## What

From the owner's testing notes of 2026-11-02, and [issue 39](https://github.com/iammrduncan/thermite/issues/39):

- Item 12: "no way on control or the dashboard to start a new campaign from another run's champion with a changed contract; had to hand-build a signed assign_campaign naming the champion's commit, with the whole contract (13 KB) inline."
- Item 17: "no way to get a run's champion out as git (issue 39): a champion patch applies to the run's seed, and [that run]'s seed is a commit Thermite made on [another rig] that [the project's repository] never got; rebuilding it needs [an earlier run]'s attempt patch, which control never received."

A run's code lives as commits in its rig's own git and as patches on control. A champion's patch applies to the run's starting commit, and that commit may exist only on the rig that made it: a run started from an earlier run's champion starts from a commit no repository has. So a champion can't be taken out as git, and a new campaign can't start from it on another rig.

1. **A branch for the run's champions.** The supervisor pushes `thermite/<run>/champion` to the project's repository: one commit for each champion, with the champion's tree, each on the one before, so the branch only moves forward. Its first commit is the run's starting point.
2. **A branch for each lane, with its work as patches.** `thermite/<run>/lane-<n>` holds the lane's attempts as patches, with what each tried and its verdict, committed as they come; not a branch for each attempt or each champion, which would soon be too many to read.
3. **A new campaign from a champion's commit.** **Start a run**, a copy of a campaign, MCP and REST start a run from another run's champion by its commit on the champion branch, with a whole new contract saved as a new version of the campaign. No hand-built `assign_campaign`.
4. **Where the results go is the creator's choice**, on **New campaign** and in the contract or assignment: branches of the project's repository (the default), or a separate results repository set from control, on every rig: the local stack, joined rigs, machines, clusters and RunPod.

## To work out

- **Write access.** Pushing needs a Git access that can push to the project's repository; today's only fetches. **New campaign** checks that it can push, as **Try it** checks a fetch, and says the results stay on control when it can't. SSH keys and HTTPS tokens.
- **Only Thermite's branches.** Only branches under `thermite/`; never the default branch or another's; a branch that already exists with other history is refused.
- **A public repository.** Pushing to a public repository publishes the agents' attempts; the page says so before the first push.
- **A project in a folder** (`project.folder`): the run's commits hold only that folder.
- **The record** (events, field notes, the knowledge base, the ledger): on control alone, or also to a results repository.
- **When to push:** with each export, as the results repository does now.
- The dashboard's part is drawn as a mockup for the owner first.

## Plan

1. **The assignment says where.** `assign_campaign` gains `results: {git, access, folder?}`: the repository to push to, the Git access that pushes, and the project's folder when it is one. None: control only. Control gives the rig the access while the assignment names it, as it does a fetch's.
2. **The supervisor builds and pushes the branches** after each export, beside its results repository's: `thermite/<run>/champion`, a commit for each champion, the seed's tree with the champion's patch applied (checked against the tree the rig published), on the one before; `thermite/<run>/lane-<n>`, a commit for each attempt adding `attempts/<nnn>-<attempt>.patch` and `.json`. A project in a folder: the champion's tree grafted into the project's commit at that folder. Fast-forwards only, refs under `thermite/<run>/` only. Its report says what is on the remote: each champion's commit, the lanes' tips, when and any error.
3. **Control shows and uses it:** **New campaign** (and the copy and Start a run drawers) choose where, with **Check it can push** (a `git push --dry-run`, which writes nothing); the run's **Champions** block shows the branch, each champion's commit and its menu (Start a campaign from it, Copy its commit, Download as a patch); **Start a campaign from #n** keeps the contract or opens New campaign filled from it; MCP and REST `start_campaign {name, from: {run, champion}, contract?}`.
4. Pages, decisions.md, tests, and the campaign acceptance check pushing to its git daemon.

## Done when

- [ ] The four, with their tests and the pages that describe them (Take the result, Set up the results repository, Exports, New campaign, Start a run), decisions.md's 2026-10-14 row replaced, and the campaign, control and judge acceptance checks pass.

## Decisions

- 2026-10-27, owner: the creator chooses where a run's results go; the default is branches of the project's own repository, one for the champion and one for each lane, named for the run; the other choice is a separate results repository (issue 39).
- 2026-11-02, owner: "branch per lane and patches for their work, main champion has a branch for the run and commits for champions. branches for each champion would get unwieldly fast."
- 2026-11-02, owner, on the [mockup](https://claude.ai/artifact/WWPowtivBuJe1ydq9VjXkf): "Build it as drawn"; the push credential is a Git access with write rights, chosen as **Push with** (only the supervisor of a rig running the campaign gets it; the harness and the agents never do); the record stays on control alone, git gets code only; built in v0.1.1, now.

## Progress

- 2026-11-02: Goal written from the owner's notes, issue 39 and the owner's design of the branches. Item 2 of goal 182 (a campaign from a champion) moved here: it needs the champion's commit in git.

